  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "A selection of Chinese poems translated by Launcelot Cranmer-Byng, with his introductory essays on Chinese verse form and the epochs of Chinese poetry. The poems are English renderings of Chinese originals, not the work of the translator alone.",
  "description": "A Feast of Lanterns is an anthology of Chinese poetry in English: the tomb inscriptions and anonymous airs of the earliest period, Tang and Song poems of parting, wine, moonlight and the frontier, and the palace verse of later dynasties, with Cranmer-Byng's essays on how Chinese verse is built and on the periods of its history. Its taste for the short lyric shaped the English reading of Chinese poetry for a generation.",
  "historicalContext": "Cranmer-Byng edited the Wisdom of the East series, in which this and his Book of Odes appeared; his translations are free, verse-paraphrases rather than literal renderings, and are presented here as English poems made from Chinese originals. The volume was published in London in 1916.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: the poems and the essays on Chinese verse, streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time, one poem or section per chapter. The editorial note, the table of contents and the advertisements at the end of the volume are not part of the work.",
